DecisionForge AI:

DecisionForge AI is a Java program that uses logic to help users make decisions based on the information they enter. The project includes two scenarios:

Scenario A – CampusRoute: Helps the user decide which transportation option is best based on their budget, schedule, weather, and preferences.

Scenario B – CoursePath: Checks whether a student meets the requirements to register for a course.

Features: The program uses Java Swing for the GUI, this allows users to interact with the program through buttons, text fields, dropdown menus, etc, instead of typing everything into the console.

The program includes:
- A main menu to choose between Scenario A and Scenario B.
- Transportation recommendations based on the user's information.
- Questions to check whether a student can register for a course.
- Truth tables to show different combinations of conditions.
- Expression trees with preorder, inorder, and postorder traversals.
- A decision tree for Scenario A.
- Knowledge graphs for both scenarios.
- Separate windows to display results and explanations.

Installation and Setup:
- Download or copy the project files from GitHub.
- Open the project in IntelliJ IDEA or another Java IDE.
- Make sure Java is installed and all the required classes are included.
- Run the GUI class in the org.example package.
- The main menu should appear, allowing you to choose between the two scenarios.

How to Use:
Scenario A – CampusRoute:
- Select Scenario A from the main menu.
- Enter your budget, departure time, arrival deadline, and weather.
- Enter the fares, walking times, travel times, and operating status for the bus, train, and backup ride.
- Choose your priorities for fare, walking time, travel time, and weather. Each priority number can only be used once.
- Click Continue to see the results.
- You can also click the buttons to view the truth tables, expression trees, decision tree, and knowledge graph.
- Example: A user has a budget of $20 and needs to get to campus by 9:00 AM. The program checks the transportation options and recommends one based on the information and priorities entered.

Scenario B – CoursePath:
- Select Scenario B from the main menu.
- Answer the four Yes/No questions about prerequisites, seat availability, registration holds, and deadlines.
- The program checks the answers to determine whether the student can register.
- The results appear in another window, showing which requirements were met and what the student should do next.
- You can also view the truth table, expression tree, knowledge graph, and verification checklist.
- Example: If a student completed the prerequisites, a seat is available, there is no registration hold, and the deadline is still open, the program will say the student is eligible to register.

Limitations:
- Scenario A uses transportation information entered by the user instead of live transportation information. So if a bus is not working or something, the program won't know until the user has updated that information.
- Scenario B is not connected to an actual university registration system. So it can't check automatically if a class still has available seats, if they have a registration hold, etc,  he user has to provide that information.
- The results depend on the information the user enters. If the user enters incorrect information, the program may give inaccurate outputs.
- Users must enter valid numbers and times for Scenario A.

